package cosmonic:agent@0.5.0;

/// The surface of one toolset component: `cosmonic:agent/tools@0.3.0`
/// exactly, except that `list` can fail with its own `list-error`. Not published; for discussion.
///
/// The tools in cosmonic-labs/sandboxed-agents#24 export nearly this shape
/// today under per-tool names (`cosmonic:iss-position/tool@0.3.0` and its
/// siblings), with one difference: their `list` fails with `call-error`, not
/// `list-error`. An implementer may adapt to that until they adopt this
/// interface. It is a sibling of `tools`,
/// so the published `tools` contract stays untouched; whether a later
/// `cosmonic:agent` version folds it into `tools` is an open question (see
/// DESIGN-NOTES.md).
///
/// One component is one toolset with its own grants: it offers one or more
/// tool definitions. `list` returns them; `call` rejects a tool name it does
/// not own with `unknown-tool` before doing any work. Nothing here routes
/// between toolsets: the implementer lists and dispatches across toolset
/// workloads, so an agent never carries a router of its own.
///
/// The toolset egresses as itself: a call is host-internal, made with the
/// toolset's own grants and workload identity. The calling agent and its
/// session are audit context, never delegated authority.
///
/// The implementer may offer a tool to the agent under an alias that is not
/// its name here (`<toolset>__<tool>` when another toolset already has the
/// name), and passes the tool's own name to `call`. So a tool's description
/// should not name sibling tools: the model may know them by other names.
/// `title` is for people; pass one when the name alone does not read well.
///
/// The implementer may bound what a call returns before the agent sees it,
/// since one oversized result would otherwise be resent on every later model
/// call. Text beyond its cap is cut at a UTF-8 boundary and followed by a
/// text part of the form `[output truncated: showing N of M bytes; narrow the
/// request]`. A structured result beyond the cap cannot be cut into valid
/// JSON, so the whole response becomes `is-error` with a text part saying it
/// was too large. An inline attachment beyond its own cap is replaced by a
/// text note. A toolset that can return a lot should take arguments that
/// narrow it.
@unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
interface tool-workload {
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    use cosmonic:agent/tools@0.3.0.{tool-def, tool-call, tool-response, call-error};

    /// Why a toolset could not list its tool definitions. A read has no
    /// effects to be unsure of, so it does not reuse `call-error`, whose
    /// dispatch and outcome cases (#71) describe calls.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    variant list-error {
        /// The toolset could not describe itself now. Retry may succeed.
        unavailable(string),
        /// A failure not captured above. Treat as permanent.
        other(option<string>),
    }

    /// This toolset's tool definitions. Fails instead of hiding a toolset
    /// that could not describe itself.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    %list: async func() -> result<list<tool-def>, list-error>;

    /// Run a tool call. Same contract as `cosmonic:agent/tools.call`.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    call: async func(request: tool-call) -> result<tool-response, call-error>;
}
